<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="NADPH repair (prokaryotes)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: NADPHX| is a hydrated form of |FRAME: NADPH|, where the hydroxyl group is attached at position 6 of the nicotinamide moiety. NAD(P)H spontaneously hydrates to both the (6S)- and (6R)- isomers. |FRAME: NADHX| and |FRAME: NADPHX| are inhibitors of several dehydrogenases |CITS: [ 9851831]| and it is thus important to eliminate them. This is achieved by a bifunctional enzyme. One of its activities, that of |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.136|, converts |FRAME: CPD0-2474| back to |FRAME: NADPH|. Since both |FRAME: CPD0-2474| and |FRAME: CPD-14133| are formed, but the enzyme can only dehydrate the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-form, the enzyme possesses a second activity, that of |FRAME: EC-5.1.99.6|, which epimerizes the &lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; form to a 60:40 ratio of &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; forms |CITS: [21994945]|. In eukaryotes the two activities are catalyzed by two different enzymes. The dehydratase is ATP-dependent, unlike the ADP-dependent prokaryotic enzyme (see |FRAME: PWY-6938|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="AU4FS-4" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
